ERIC POON: Thanks, Jagan.

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:21.970
So first and foremost, why HPC,
and why do we run it on GCP?

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:24.910
Well, you guys already know why
you need to have HPC workloads.

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:27.280
We're here to tell you
why do you want it on GCP.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:30.247
But just a little
bit of recap anyway.

00:02:30.247 --> 00:02:31.330
Compute needs are growing.

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:33.310
And I don't think it's
a surprise to anyone

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:35.500
that it's growing in this way.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:38.140
Here's a growth chart of
the top 500 supercomputers,

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:41.430
with the sum of the list
now exceeding one exaflop,

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:43.895
and exascale systems are
actually on their way.

00:02:43.895 --> 00:02:45.520
So while these are
the leading systems,

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:47.680
the rest of the HPC
industry follows along

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:50.420
with the same trends.

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:52.530
And on the data side--
not just compute side,

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:55.118
right, with HPC, we
also talk about data.

00:02:55.118 --> 00:02:57.410
"Big data," we've been using
that term for a long time.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:01.490
10 years ago, when we had
3-petabyte-capacity systems,

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:03.140
we called that big data.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:06.500
Now we're at
250-petabytes-capacity systems,

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:08.760
10 years later, and we're
still calling that big data.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:10.820
So big data is actually
bigger than big.

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:18.330
And the third variable in all
this, it's also expensive.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:21.110
Look at all those numbers on
that timeline on the left side.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:27.030
And running on-prem, we
also have that limitation

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:29.202
of how we can consume.

00:03:29.202 --> 00:03:30.660
The time that we
need our workloads

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.840
to run and be processed
is not really predictable.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:35.490
Sometimes it's bursty.

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:39.300
And with on-prem,
with 100%, we're

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:44.740
really forced to wait
when we get to this area.

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:48.090
If I run out of compute and
I have to put more things in,

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:49.900
I have to wait until
it's available.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.235
And when I don't have
a lot of things to run,

00:03:52.235 --> 00:03:53.610
I'm wasting money
because I still

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:57.150
have to pay for that power
to run my on-prem systems.

00:03:57.150 --> 00:04:00.930
So running it in the cloud
is a pretty good idea

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:02.190
in our opinion.

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:04.110
But what are the
key differentiators

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:06.750
for Google, and why do
we want to run on Google?

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:09.780
Compute storage and networking,
those three main things.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:12.547
On the compute side of things,
as you guys all probably know,

00:04:12.547 --> 00:04:14.130
how many of you know
that we have what

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:16.899
we call preemptible VMs?

00:04:16.899 --> 00:04:18.550
These are VMs that
we haven't sold yet,

00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:19.839
and we're like, well,
they're sitting there anyway,

00:04:19.839 --> 00:04:21.610
why don't we give it
to you at a discount?

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:23.943
They're super-low cost and
they're short-term instances.

00:04:23.943 --> 00:04:25.750
And in order to
make sure that you

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:29.158
don't use those preemptible
VMs in a bad way,

00:04:29.158 --> 00:04:30.700
we actually say,
you know what, we're

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:33.847
going to preempt them
at least once every day.

00:04:33.847 --> 00:04:36.430
This way, you don't get used to
having preemptible VMs running

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:39.565
long term and then, all
of a sudden, they go away.

00:04:39.565 --> 00:04:40.440
But what's coming up?

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:42.020
What's new?

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:45.470
We do have some new VM families
that are available in the AP,

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:47.630
and that's
compute-optimized VMs.

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:50.970
Anyone interested in
Cascade Lake processors?

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:52.780
3.8 gigahertz, right?

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:54.930
That's coming in
compute-optimized VMs.

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:58.650
You just heard that
announced during Next today.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:00.330
We have memory-optimized VMs.

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:02.070
This way, we can
actually have 6 terabyte

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:06.360
and 12 terabyte memory attached
to VMs in your processing

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:07.090
nodes.

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:10.165
So this is the exciting
part of what's coming.

00:05:10.165 --> 00:05:12.540
So if we look at, across the
board, of what we've already

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:16.030
brought a while ago--
custom machine types,

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:18.200
what we brought a
while ago, preemptible

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:22.360
VMs, quick, point-to-point
VM-to-VM networks.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:24.550
And tomorrow, we are bringing
compute-optimized VMs.

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:28.140
We're bringing native job
scheduler integrations,

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:31.030
sole tenant nodes for those
chatty workloads that you have,

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:35.290
and most importantly, bringing
all of this from VM clusters

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:36.880
to Kubernetes.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:40.610
All of this is
coming technology.

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:42.890
Storage is also a thing.

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:45.980
Most of the time, when
you are running on-prem,

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:49.280
you're talking about
different data stores.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:54.950
They typically evolve around a
shared file system or other--

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:55.800
what else is there?

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:59.000
What else do you guys
run on-prem for storage?

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:00.440
Lustre, Elastifile.

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:04.340
We are partnering with all
of these different vendors

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:05.060
out there--

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:10.770
storage vendors-- to bring you a
good storage ecosystem as well.

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:15.320
And later, we'll see a whole
slide of those partners.

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:19.090
And last but not least,
the network on GCP.

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:20.830
We have a fast network.

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:26.710
We have 16 terabit
cross-section network bandwidth

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:28.540
inside our data centers.

00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:33.600
And you can actually have
up to 7,000 VMs per VPC.

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:35.890
There's predictable
latency across any node

00:06:35.890 --> 00:06:38.590
across the globe.

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:41.500
And each VM can
actually take advantage

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:45.130
of 16 gigabits of throughput.

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:46.560
So this is our ecosystem.

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:50.483
Between storage, workload
managers, HPC applications,

00:06:50.483 --> 00:06:51.900
and solutions
integrators that can

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:57.072
help you get your workload
there, I believe HPC on GCP

00:06:57.072 --> 00:06:57.780
is the way to go.

00:07:01.392 --> 00:07:02.850
Feel free to take
a picture of this

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:05.490
as well for a link to any of
the other sessions that are

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:13.550
HPC-related here at Next 2019.

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:15.050
So now we know why, but how?

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:15.750
How do we do it?

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:17.930
Well, what are some of the
blueprints that we have?

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:19.430
What are the common
patterns that we

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:22.670
see HPC customers using on GCP?

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:25.370
Burst to cloud-- you
can do auto-scaling.

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:27.710
I don't always need to
have a static cluster

00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:29.190
of a certain size.

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:31.850
And if I am streaming
input to my workload,

00:07:31.850 --> 00:07:35.150
I can actually have that cluster
grow and shrink as needed,

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:38.120
so I am paying for only
the compute that I need,

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:41.390
and not a cent more.

00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:44.030
We can actually also federate
your compute workload

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:45.830
to the cloud only.

00:07:45.830 --> 00:07:48.398
Running in this way over
VPN or interconnects,

00:07:48.398 --> 00:07:49.940
you can actually
have your schedulers

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:52.400
and your worker nodes
on-prem, and only when

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:54.320
your on-prem
hardware is exhausted

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:57.240
and you need to go faster do
we need to go to the cloud.

00:07:57.240 --> 00:08:00.620
So this is another
pattern that we see.

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:04.890
So putting it all together,
we have a lot of things here.

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:07.230
Number one, we
see external feeds

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:11.280
and cloud Pub/Sub as an
example of a streaming input

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:12.090
to Cloud Storage.

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:14.340
And Cloud Storage is our
product where we really

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:20.600
stage all the inputs and the big
data to be used in these jobs.

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:23.642
That goes in to your HPC farm.

00:08:23.642 --> 00:08:25.100
On Compute Engine,
you can actually

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:28.713
have GPUs, TPUs,
as well as CPUs.

00:08:28.713 --> 00:08:30.630
And this is the part
that we're talking about.

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:32.630
This is the part that
can scale up and down

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:36.650
and run compute
optimized, et cetera.

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:39.049
The rest of the diagram
that you see here

00:08:39.049 --> 00:08:41.340
are really integrations
with other things.

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:43.220
So if you take a
look at number 4,

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:46.040
after the HPC perform
processes everything

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:48.500
and it puts it back
into a big storage area,

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:51.050
we're going to once again make
use of Google Cloud Storage.

00:08:51.050 --> 00:08:53.720
And from there, you can actually
hook that up to BigQuery,

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:56.630
put that in archival
storage and code line,

00:08:56.630 --> 00:09:00.290
use that to train ML
models in TensorFlow.

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:05.550
When you run HPC on GCP, the
entire ecosystem opens to you,

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:08.030
and that's what
we're talking about.

00:09:08.030 --> 00:09:11.050
With that said, how can
we help you get there?

00:09:14.802 --> 00:09:16.010
JAGAN CUDDAPAH: Thanks, Eric.

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:19.890
So as you saw in the last slide,
we have a lot of products.

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:23.660
And I had mentioned earlier on
that the Google ecosystem is

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:25.227
constantly growing.

00:09:25.227 --> 00:09:27.560
It's great, and it's also
challenging in the perspective

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:31.850
that you have to learn a lot
of new things on the fly,

00:09:31.850 --> 00:09:33.870
basically at all times,
which is a good thing.

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:35.412
But when you try to
build a solution,

00:09:35.412 --> 00:09:38.313
it sort of gets
overwhelming so we,

00:09:38.313 --> 00:09:39.980
at Google professional
services, sort of

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:41.780
believe in building with you.

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:45.050
The idea is that our HPC SMEs--

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:46.780
some of them are
with you right now,

00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:48.800
our consultants who
you're going to hear from,

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:52.640
and cloud engineers
like Eric himself--

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:55.820
we will work with you
to build this out.

00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:58.540
The idea is not like
other consulting firms,

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:02.130
wherein we just come in, and
we just do stuff and go away.

00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:04.980
We believe in this thing
called crawl, walk, run.

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:07.940
It's a well-understood
term in the industry.

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:11.420
But basically the idea
is, pace yourself,

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:13.020
as opposed to try
to do-- everybody

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:14.270
wants to do something radical.

00:10:14.270 --> 00:10:16.910
Everybody wants to do something
really cool and innovative.

00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:21.380
But we want to try to get
something practical done first,

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:23.210
and then you sort of
go to the next step.

00:10:23.210 --> 00:10:25.190
So from an HPC
perspective, what we

00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:26.690
have done with other
customers where

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:29.328
we've been quite successful
is, first we come in

00:10:29.328 --> 00:10:30.870
and we do some sort
of questionnaire,

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:33.207
and we work with
your HPC engineers

00:10:33.207 --> 00:10:34.790
to understand the
current state-- what

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:36.873
batch schedulers do you
have, what storage systems

00:10:36.873 --> 00:10:37.652
do you use?

00:10:37.652 --> 00:10:39.110
Then we try to
understand what that

00:10:39.110 --> 00:10:42.200
means, like what do you want
to change in six months?

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:44.750
Then we develop a
target state for you.

00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:47.780
And then we actually
work with you

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:50.037
to build a proof of concept.

00:10:50.037 --> 00:10:51.620
This is a pretty
interesting exercise,

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:52.580
and I'll talk to you
a little bit more

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:54.290
about it in the
next slide, but it's

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:56.420
when Google engineers
actually sit down

00:10:56.420 --> 00:11:00.470
with your HPC staff
and your business folks

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:04.880
and we work with you to
deploy your workload on GCP--

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:09.092
how to set up the network,
how to configure IM policies.

00:11:09.092 --> 00:11:10.550
There's a whole
bunch of stuff that

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:13.100
has to happen which we're
going to help you with.

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:15.110
The second piece
is when you walk.

00:11:15.110 --> 00:11:17.990
Now that you know the POC and
you have confidence in it,

00:11:17.990 --> 00:11:20.490
what you're really looking to
do is to actually deploy this.

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:22.790
So Google Professional
Services can

00:11:22.790 --> 00:11:26.510
help, but we have a whole bunch
of partners, like ROs and GFD--

00:11:26.510 --> 00:11:28.700
in the slide, you saw
that-- who will actually

00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:31.010
do this work for you if you
don't have the bandwidth

00:11:31.010 --> 00:11:32.000
to do this.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:33.923
Typically we've seen
customers go either way.

00:11:33.923 --> 00:11:35.090
If you want to do it, great.

00:11:35.090 --> 00:11:37.173
If you don't want to do
it, then we have partners.

00:11:37.173 --> 00:11:38.630
We can do it either ways.

00:11:38.630 --> 00:11:40.910
And then the final state is run.

00:11:40.910 --> 00:11:43.280
And this is where it gets
really exciting for customers.

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:45.980
Now that you are in the cloud
and you have this deployed,

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:47.965
how do you make the
best use of the cloud?

00:11:47.965 --> 00:11:50.090
So the architecture that
Eric was showing you about

00:11:50.090 --> 00:11:51.167
is cloud native.

00:11:51.167 --> 00:11:53.000
But that's basically
the power of the cloud,

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:56.400
is you get to try all
these new products.

00:11:56.400 --> 00:12:00.730
So with that, today, we are
launching a new service.

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:03.360
We call it the first part
of the journey, the crawl

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:04.890
part of the journey.

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:07.020
It's called Cloud Discover HPC.

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:09.570
This is a service
offering that we have been

00:12:09.570 --> 00:12:12.110
piloting for the last one year.

00:12:12.110 --> 00:12:14.127
We've been fairly
successful at it.

00:12:14.127 --> 00:12:15.960
We've done it with a
financial services firm

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:16.890
that you'll hear from.

00:12:16.890 --> 00:12:18.602
We've done it with
a health care firm.

00:12:18.602 --> 00:12:21.060
We're doing it with another
big health care firm next week,

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:22.768
and we've done it with
[? 3Du. ?] We have

00:12:22.768 --> 00:12:25.320
done about like 78 of these--

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:27.490
some with us, some
with our partners.

00:12:27.490 --> 00:12:29.700
We've gotten pretty good
at it, I would think.

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:33.150
The idea is that, in the very
first week, what we're doing is

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:35.497
we do some prep work before
we get on the ground,

00:12:35.497 --> 00:12:37.080
to understand what
your workload looks

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:38.730
like and all that good stuff.

00:12:38.730 --> 00:12:40.560
Then we actually do
a whiteboarding--

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:43.440
then we come on site, on a
three-day effort, wherein

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:46.590
we sit down with you, we
do whiteboarding exercises

00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:48.635
to figure out what
the target state is.

00:12:48.635 --> 00:12:50.010
And as soon as
you do that, we're

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:52.740
actually in code
development mode.

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:55.800
We are actually getting our
hands dirty with your staff.

00:12:55.800 --> 00:13:01.440
And the idea is that, in three
days, we want to build a POC.

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:03.173
And also it's not
like we do the work

00:13:03.173 --> 00:13:04.340
and you sit out of the room.

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:05.898
We're actually sitting with you.

00:13:05.898 --> 00:13:07.440
And the idea is, we
want to teach you

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:09.930
how to do this as
opposed to us do this.

00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:12.360
Once this is done, we go
back, we understand what's

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:15.690
been done so far, then we put
together a technical design

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:19.020
document, which is
essentially a plan that says,

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:21.450
OK, now that this is the POC,
how do you take this POC,

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:23.640
and how do you take
it to the next level.

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:25.470
It's a pretty good
exercise, because what

00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:27.520
we have seen
customers do is they

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:30.020
get their hands dirty with GCP
and all the different product

00:13:30.020 --> 00:13:33.570
sets out there, as opposed to
just being a bunch of slides.

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:36.900
And the second thing is, you get
to work with real-life stuff,

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:40.560
which is always exciting.

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:43.320
I just described this to you,
but just to go back on that,

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:47.030
week 0 is very much
around preparation,

00:13:47.030 --> 00:13:51.510
week 1 is planning, and then
week 2 is design for future.

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:53.670
One thing I definitely
want to call out is,

00:13:53.670 --> 00:13:55.530
we've had a lot of
customers do this,

00:13:55.530 --> 00:13:59.940
and the part that really excites
them is that when the business

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:02.760
comes up with a use case--
let's say bulk calculations are

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:05.365
getting slower, we need more
capacity, whatever it is--

00:14:05.365 --> 00:14:06.990
this is a great
opportunity for your IT

00:14:06.990 --> 00:14:08.440
to get involved as well.

00:14:08.440 --> 00:14:10.020
And we actually
sit with your IT.

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:11.820
And in a lot of
enterprise organizations,

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:13.560
the business and IT
are two separate--

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:15.143
I don't want to say
silos, but they're

00:14:15.143 --> 00:14:18.390
two different organizations
with two different priorities.

00:14:18.390 --> 00:14:21.180
So this is one area where the
business has said something,

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:25.010
and within five days, you
can say, hey, we built a POC,

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:27.270
this works or this doesn't--
hopefully it works.

00:14:27.270 --> 00:14:29.840
And we can say OK, now what?

00:14:29.840 --> 00:14:33.800
So I know I've said a lot
about how good we are.

00:14:33.800 --> 00:14:37.220
I think it'll be helpful
for you to hear from one

00:14:37.220 --> 00:14:39.670
of our first customers.

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:41.990
So I'd like to call
on stage Naveed Malik.

00:14:41.990 --> 00:14:45.200
He's the head of data
for Cubist Systematic.

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:47.000
He was one of our
first customers.

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:51.600
And Naveed will share the
good and the bad that we had.

00:14:55.400 --> 00:14:58.360
NAVEED MALIK: Thank you, Jagan.

00:14:58.360 --> 00:15:01.020
First of all, there are
glaring lights in front of me.

00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:03.410
So if I'm not looking at
you, I'm not ignoring you,

00:15:03.410 --> 00:15:05.420
I'm just trying to
avoid the lights.

00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:24.010
So starting with the high-level
overview of our company,

00:15:24.010 --> 00:15:27.715
Cubist Systematic is the
trading division of Point72.

00:15:27.715 --> 00:15:31.000
And Point72 is one of the
legendary hedge funds, founded

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:35.130
by Steve Cohen in 1992.

00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:39.520
The systematic business
was started early 2000s.

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:42.165
And the systematic business
is now called Cubist.

00:15:47.540 --> 00:15:52.790
So when we talk about
data, any hedge fund

00:15:52.790 --> 00:15:54.470
typically uses a lot of data.

00:15:54.470 --> 00:15:57.710
But specifically, the
systematic hedge funds--

00:15:57.710 --> 00:15:59.360
other names are like
black box trading,

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:02.960
or whatever you call it,
or quantitative trading--

00:16:02.960 --> 00:16:05.150
they use massive
amounts of data,

00:16:05.150 --> 00:16:09.080
because they need this data to
run their trading strategies,

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:10.925
because these are 100%
automated systems.

00:16:14.290 --> 00:16:17.210
We started thinking about
HPC a few years ago.

00:16:17.210 --> 00:16:21.940
At that time, Google Cloud
or Google HPC environment

00:16:21.940 --> 00:16:23.770
was not at that level.

00:16:23.770 --> 00:16:25.750
We tried a few
other options, which

00:16:25.750 --> 00:16:28.730
is out-of-scope for
today's discussion.

00:16:28.730 --> 00:16:32.260
But I'll talk a little
bit about where we went

00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:34.100
and how we set it up.

00:16:34.100 --> 00:16:38.780
So in general, any data
processing problem,

00:16:38.780 --> 00:16:40.930
first of all, it is
going to be I/O bound.

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:43.030
So a lot of people
make the mistake

00:16:43.030 --> 00:16:45.190
of throwing a lot
of codes at it.

00:16:45.190 --> 00:16:46.850
It's not going to
solve the problem.

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:48.760
Because if you are
I/O bound, first you

00:16:48.760 --> 00:16:51.680
need to solve the I/O problem--

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:52.645
I/O and network.

00:16:56.310 --> 00:16:59.010
So the way we solve
the problem is

00:16:59.010 --> 00:17:05.430
by building an in-house
HPC environment, which

00:17:05.430 --> 00:17:10.319
utilizes a cluster-based
solution using cheap

00:17:10.319 --> 00:17:15.200
magnetic disks with
SSDs for metadata,

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:19.470
and connected through
the InfiniBand fabric

00:17:19.470 --> 00:17:20.970
to compute nodes.

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:24.910
And compute nodes are
running Condor grid.

00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:28.980
So this environment works pretty
neat for our requirements.

00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:33.200
Actually, at that time, when we
were building this environment

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:35.830
about three years ago,
we knew there are going

00:17:35.830 --> 00:17:38.160
to be some bursty workloads.

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:40.350
We thought, OK, it will
take us a week to--

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:44.520
from time to time, to run
through those bursty workloads.

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:48.990
But for sustained workloads,
the in-house HPC was enough.

00:17:48.990 --> 00:17:51.810
And sustained workloads are
what we had to do, day in

00:17:51.810 --> 00:17:54.720
and day out, in terms
of processing the data

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:57.462
as well as delivering
the data to the clients.

00:17:57.462 --> 00:17:59.420
And I'll have a little
bit more detail on that.

00:18:05.010 --> 00:18:09.317
So question is, why did
we choose Google Cloud?

00:18:09.317 --> 00:18:11.150
And actually, even more
fundamental question

00:18:11.150 --> 00:18:14.980
is, why did we choose
to go for cloud?

00:18:14.980 --> 00:18:18.930
So as I mentioned earlier,
the in-house HPC solution

00:18:18.930 --> 00:18:21.470
was great for our
sustained workloads.

00:18:21.470 --> 00:18:23.370
But the bursty workloads
that we thought

00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:25.287
are going to be occasional
actually turned out

00:18:25.287 --> 00:18:26.565
to be a lot more frequent.

00:18:26.565 --> 00:18:30.810
It happens maybe once a
week, a few times a month,

00:18:30.810 --> 00:18:33.450
where we have to go through
some massive amount of data

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:36.090
processing.

00:18:36.090 --> 00:18:37.980
And that creates
a problem for us,

00:18:37.980 --> 00:18:42.210
because in that time, first
of all, the users are waiting.

00:18:42.210 --> 00:18:46.440
If a user has to wait for, say,
seven days to get their data,

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:47.850
that is an opportunity lost.

00:18:47.850 --> 00:18:50.700
They could have done something
else during that time.

00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:55.070
So to deal with
those situations,

00:18:55.070 --> 00:18:57.990
we considered other options.

00:18:57.990 --> 00:19:01.110
And then we decided
to run a complete POC

00:19:01.110 --> 00:19:05.550
after getting an overview
of storage, and network,

00:19:05.550 --> 00:19:08.040
and compute resources
provided by Google.

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:08.910
So that is--

00:19:18.010 --> 00:19:20.220
So as I mentioned
earlier, when we

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:22.717
are talking about
HPC environments,

00:19:22.717 --> 00:19:24.300
there are a few
fundamental components

00:19:24.300 --> 00:19:25.680
that you have to worry about--

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:29.130
storage and network are
the most important one.

00:19:29.130 --> 00:19:32.550
And I'm talking about
HPC for data processing.

00:19:32.550 --> 00:19:35.250
There might be other situations
where you just need compute,

00:19:35.250 --> 00:19:36.660
and you're good with it.

00:19:36.660 --> 00:19:38.310
But for data
processing workloads,

00:19:38.310 --> 00:19:39.900
you need to have
good storage which

00:19:39.900 --> 00:19:43.310
can supply enough
data to your nodes,

00:19:43.310 --> 00:19:46.320
and network, which
can deliver the data--

00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:48.180
and compute, obviously.

00:19:48.180 --> 00:19:50.020
But there are two
additional components,

00:19:50.020 --> 00:19:54.390
which we were very conscious
about and I'm sure a lot of you

00:19:54.390 --> 00:19:55.910
are going to be conscious.

00:19:55.910 --> 00:19:57.540
They are cost and support.

00:19:57.540 --> 00:20:04.830
So cost-- a lot of people
think that cloud is cheap.

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:07.850
But if you don't use
it in a sensible way,

00:20:07.850 --> 00:20:09.720
it can become very expensive.

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:13.710
So we were very
cautious about the cost.

00:20:13.710 --> 00:20:17.980
We wanted to be
sure, first of all,

00:20:17.980 --> 00:20:19.860
how the storage is
going to perform,

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:22.750
how is the network
going to be, what

00:20:22.750 --> 00:20:25.760
are the costs for the
compute, and overall,

00:20:25.760 --> 00:20:27.830
what is the cost going to be?

00:20:27.830 --> 00:20:31.162
And support-- in any kind
of compute environment,

00:20:31.162 --> 00:20:32.620
you want to have
very good support.

00:20:36.190 --> 00:20:42.640
So, speaking of support,
as Jagan mentioned earlier,

00:20:42.640 --> 00:20:44.980
this PSO organization was great.

00:20:44.980 --> 00:20:49.450
It was a fantastic team of
engineers and developers.

00:20:49.450 --> 00:20:52.570
And Gabriele, in
his leadership, he

00:20:52.570 --> 00:20:54.160
brought together
a really good team

00:20:54.160 --> 00:20:56.533
which helped us set
up this environment.

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:04.950
So now I'll go a little bit into
more detail on the application

00:21:04.950 --> 00:21:08.190
or the problem which was
for testing the Google HPC

00:21:08.190 --> 00:21:09.870
environment.

00:21:09.870 --> 00:21:16.370
So we chose to use one of the
problems, which is level 2 data

00:21:16.370 --> 00:21:17.540
processing.

00:21:17.540 --> 00:21:20.802
And specifically we chose CME.

00:21:20.802 --> 00:21:22.760
This was just one of the
more established ones,

00:21:22.760 --> 00:21:24.940
so we wanted to try that one.

00:21:24.940 --> 00:21:29.330
In adaptive book processing,
you get massive amount

00:21:29.330 --> 00:21:31.760
of data in terms of
updates to the book.

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:36.078
And every update to the
book results in a change

00:21:36.078 --> 00:21:37.120
of the state of the book.

00:21:40.730 --> 00:21:42.950
And more importantly,
every method

00:21:42.950 --> 00:21:46.140
had to be processed in
a particular sequence.

00:21:46.140 --> 00:21:49.115
So you have the option to--

00:21:49.115 --> 00:21:52.180
the first logical thing is
maybe save the state of the book

00:21:52.180 --> 00:21:54.630
every time there is a change.

00:21:54.630 --> 00:21:57.650
The problem is there are a
large number of instruments,

00:21:57.650 --> 00:22:01.530
and every instrument can tick
tens or hundreds of times

00:22:01.530 --> 00:22:02.760
every millisecond.

00:22:02.760 --> 00:22:07.710
So if you try to save the
book state at every update,

00:22:07.710 --> 00:22:10.080
it will result in a
gigantic amount of data.

00:22:10.080 --> 00:22:12.720
And there is nothing wrong
with gigantic amount of data.

00:22:12.720 --> 00:22:14.400
The problem is, to
retrieve it, it's

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:17.690
going to be counterproductive.

00:22:17.690 --> 00:22:23.010
In this case, CME is a 50
gigabyte compressed messages,

00:22:23.010 --> 00:22:24.260
not a book state.

00:22:24.260 --> 00:22:26.570
So compressed
messages, every day,

00:22:26.570 --> 00:22:28.450
and we had to go through
12 years of history.

00:22:33.480 --> 00:22:35.030
So what was the
business objective

00:22:35.030 --> 00:22:37.640
of this particular application?

00:22:37.640 --> 00:22:41.210
The business objective is to
be able to deliver book state

00:22:41.210 --> 00:22:46.050
for any given instrument at any
given point in time in history.

00:22:46.050 --> 00:22:47.900
So as I mentioned,
the messages have

00:22:47.900 --> 00:22:51.350
to be processed in a
sequence, from the opening

00:22:51.350 --> 00:22:54.290
time to the point in
time the user needs it.

00:22:54.290 --> 00:22:56.930
So it is very expensive to
go through the whole days

00:22:56.930 --> 00:23:02.100
of messages to build a state
for the given point in time.

00:23:02.100 --> 00:23:06.110
So what we did is we ran
through the 12 years of history,

00:23:06.110 --> 00:23:08.990
saved a snapshot of
the book every minute

00:23:08.990 --> 00:23:13.580
boundary, and we have an
API which recreates the book

00:23:13.580 --> 00:23:15.210
at any given point in time.

00:23:15.210 --> 00:23:19.410
So the user requests,
for instrument ABC,

00:23:19.410 --> 00:23:22.280
I need data as of
this timestamp--

00:23:22.280 --> 00:23:24.650
for the book state
as of this timestamp.

00:23:24.650 --> 00:23:28.910
It finds the previous
minute snapshot,

00:23:28.910 --> 00:23:32.000
and applies the messages
during that minute,

00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:34.430
and builds and
delivers the state.

00:23:34.430 --> 00:23:36.410
It does a lot more
other things also

00:23:36.410 --> 00:23:39.121
which are out-of-scope
for this discussion.

00:23:45.510 --> 00:23:48.920
So doing this, the
result was we were

00:23:48.920 --> 00:23:54.350
able to successfully duplicate
our environment in Google,

00:23:54.350 --> 00:23:58.970
and not only duplicate, we were
able to test the scalability

00:23:58.970 --> 00:23:59.820
also.

00:23:59.820 --> 00:24:02.770
As I mentioned
earlier in the slides,

00:24:02.770 --> 00:24:05.660
the first challenge is,
always, for data processing,

00:24:05.660 --> 00:24:07.490
think about storage and network.

00:24:07.490 --> 00:24:09.385
We were OK with that.

00:24:09.385 --> 00:24:10.760
And then the
problem that we were

00:24:10.760 --> 00:24:15.170
having being able to scale
up and down on demand,

00:24:15.170 --> 00:24:16.010
that was resolved.

00:24:19.570 --> 00:24:24.850
And we tested by spinning up 400
compute nodes in-house anytime

00:24:24.850 --> 00:24:28.850
we are running from
20 to 30 nodes.

00:24:28.850 --> 00:24:30.890
And we did some
experimentation and there is

00:24:30.890 --> 00:24:33.050
some additional
experiments going on,

00:24:33.050 --> 00:24:36.050
in terms of how much
we can push it--

00:24:36.050 --> 00:24:39.140
can we run 1,000 and 2,000, and
how does the performance scale?

00:24:42.100 --> 00:24:46.280
As like with any
successful test,

00:24:46.280 --> 00:24:50.500
a good customer service
would ask for feedback.

00:24:50.500 --> 00:24:53.720
And Jagan and Gabriele
asked me kind of definitely,

00:24:53.720 --> 00:24:57.140
what should we improve, what
we could have done better.

00:24:57.140 --> 00:25:01.630
So as I mentioned,
for any client,

00:25:01.630 --> 00:25:04.675
cost is definitely one of
the factors to worry about.

00:25:04.675 --> 00:25:06.760
And especially with
the preemptibles,

00:25:06.760 --> 00:25:08.480
that's a great idea.

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:09.300
I love the idea.

00:25:09.300 --> 00:25:10.840
The problem is,
it's hard to tell

00:25:10.840 --> 00:25:13.960
how many preemptibles are going
to be available at any given

00:25:13.960 --> 00:25:15.170
time.

00:25:15.170 --> 00:25:21.230
So calculating costs becomes a
much more complicated problem.

00:25:21.230 --> 00:25:23.390
You can hope there is going
to be low cost because

00:25:23.390 --> 00:25:25.415
of preemptibles, but
you don't have an idea

00:25:25.415 --> 00:25:26.780
of what it is going to be.

00:25:26.780 --> 00:25:31.460
So coming from a
quantitative background,

00:25:31.460 --> 00:25:34.760
my suggestion is to make
either the history available

00:25:34.760 --> 00:25:38.810
or just use the history, run
some ML techniques on top of it

00:25:38.810 --> 00:25:41.300
to kind of predict how many
preemptibles are going to get

00:25:41.300 --> 00:25:43.420
utilized for a given problem.

00:25:43.420 --> 00:25:45.870
So during the PSA,
we actually had

00:25:45.870 --> 00:25:50.050
to run the batches to
see the actual cost--

00:25:50.050 --> 00:25:53.900
like after running it, instead
of trying estimate it ahead

00:25:53.900 --> 00:25:55.020
of time.

00:25:55.020 --> 00:25:57.910
Although kudos to
Google for providing

00:25:57.910 --> 00:26:00.260
an excellent dashboard
for looking at what

00:26:00.260 --> 00:26:03.360
the cost is, where
the cost came from,

00:26:03.360 --> 00:26:07.240
whether it came from storage,
network, or from compute.

00:26:07.240 --> 00:26:09.500
And just so you know,
most of the cost

00:26:09.500 --> 00:26:12.528
was coming from storage
because we wanted

00:26:12.528 --> 00:26:13.820
a very high-throughput storage.

00:26:17.710 --> 00:26:19.880
