00:27:47.020 --> 00:27:49.180
All right, can we
switch to the demo?

00:27:55.928 --> 00:27:58.240
All right, thanks a lot.

00:27:58.240 --> 00:28:01.280
All right, in the
next 10 minutes or so,

00:28:01.280 --> 00:28:04.750
I'm going to show you how
to deploy an HTCondor batch

00:28:04.750 --> 00:28:06.520
scheduler in GCP.

00:28:06.520 --> 00:28:09.190
So HTCondor is the
batch scheduler

00:28:09.190 --> 00:28:12.950
that we deployed at Cubist, so
I thought it was a good example

00:28:12.950 --> 00:28:15.430
to show you.

00:28:15.430 --> 00:28:18.610
It's not the only scheduler
that we support in GCP.

00:28:18.610 --> 00:28:21.850
There is Slurm, PBS,
there is a whole bunch.

00:28:24.520 --> 00:28:26.770
What we will be doing
now, I will show you

00:28:26.770 --> 00:28:31.540
how to deploy this using Google
Deployment Manager scripts.

00:28:31.540 --> 00:28:33.700
So I'll show you where
to get these scripts,

00:28:33.700 --> 00:28:36.820
how to launch them,
and then the scripts

00:28:36.820 --> 00:28:40.660
will deploy what we call a
submit host, where you can

00:28:40.660 --> 00:28:42.500
log in and submit your jobs.

00:28:42.500 --> 00:28:44.170
There will be a
Central Manager that

00:28:44.170 --> 00:28:47.830
will be used to
coordinate and match

00:28:47.830 --> 00:28:50.110
the jobs to the resources.

00:28:53.898 --> 00:28:55.940
Once I have showed you
how to deploy the cluster,

00:28:55.940 --> 00:28:59.510
I will show you how to set up
auto-scaling for your cluster

00:28:59.510 --> 00:29:02.780
with a solution that
will bring up nodes

00:29:02.780 --> 00:29:06.080
or down depending on
the number of jobs that

00:29:06.080 --> 00:29:07.950
are available in the queue.

00:29:07.950 --> 00:29:09.620
So let's start.

00:29:09.620 --> 00:29:11.930
First thing, I'm going
to get some software.

00:29:11.930 --> 00:29:13.580
So I'll start from here.

00:29:13.580 --> 00:29:18.350
It's software that you
can find in GitHub--

00:29:18.350 --> 00:29:20.318
GCP and HTCondor.

00:29:20.318 --> 00:29:21.860
So these are the
three words that you

00:29:21.860 --> 00:29:23.870
need to find the software.

00:29:23.870 --> 00:29:26.030
And look here,
I'm feeling lucky,

00:29:26.030 --> 00:29:29.660
and the first hit
is my software.

00:29:29.660 --> 00:29:31.700
Now, everything that
I'm going to show you

00:29:31.700 --> 00:29:34.250
is actually explained in here.

00:29:34.250 --> 00:29:37.080
So you can actually
redo it yourself.

00:29:37.080 --> 00:29:40.700
But of course, I'm
going to show you now.

00:29:40.700 --> 00:29:43.520
To get the software--
so this is GitHub.

00:29:43.520 --> 00:29:47.660
You can go to the repository.

00:29:47.660 --> 00:29:55.140
You can say, Clone, copy the
GitHub link into your memory,

00:29:55.140 --> 00:29:59.450
and then you can say
git clone, and here.

00:29:59.450 --> 00:30:01.770
This will bring the software
into your Cloud Shell

00:30:01.770 --> 00:30:02.390
environment.

00:30:02.390 --> 00:30:04.490
I'm showing you my
Cloud Shell environment.

00:30:04.490 --> 00:30:08.872
I've created a project called
HTCondor Demo to do that.

00:30:08.872 --> 00:30:10.580
And then of course,
since this is a demo,

00:30:10.580 --> 00:30:12.520
I have already downloaded it.

00:30:12.520 --> 00:30:16.460
And so I can go to
where the code is.

00:30:21.180 --> 00:30:23.030
So this is the
software that we're

00:30:23.030 --> 00:30:26.030
going to use to
provision the resources

00:30:26.030 --> 00:30:29.690
and then run startup
scripts that will

00:30:29.690 --> 00:30:31.595
install HP Condor in the nodes.

00:30:34.610 --> 00:30:38.140
This is the main
piece of software

00:30:38.140 --> 00:30:40.810
that you need to configure
to change the configuration

00:30:40.810 --> 00:30:43.630
of your cluster.

00:30:43.630 --> 00:30:45.430
It's the Condor cluster YAML.

00:30:45.430 --> 00:30:48.160
So this is the list of
files that you have,

00:30:48.160 --> 00:30:50.980
this is what you want to
change, and the parameters

00:30:50.980 --> 00:30:52.570
are these ones.

00:30:52.570 --> 00:30:55.300
You have the number
of nodes that you

00:30:55.300 --> 00:30:57.670
want to have statically
located, then

00:30:57.670 --> 00:31:01.150
you can scale them up and down.

00:31:01.150 --> 00:31:04.690
The type of resources that
you want to provision.

00:31:04.690 --> 00:31:06.340
In this case, I'm
provisioning nodes

00:31:06.340 --> 00:31:08.860
with four calls each, CentOS.

00:31:08.860 --> 00:31:12.370
And then Condor version
8.81, which is the latest.

00:31:12.370 --> 00:31:15.345
I know this demo
works with this.

00:31:15.345 --> 00:31:16.720
If you don't
specify the version,

00:31:16.720 --> 00:31:18.470
you get the latest one.

00:31:18.470 --> 00:31:21.890
So let's get down to it.

00:31:21.890 --> 00:31:30.517
So to kick it off,
you use this command,

00:31:30.517 --> 00:31:32.350
which was also explained
in the instructions

00:31:32.350 --> 00:31:35.000
as I was showing
it to you before.

00:31:35.000 --> 00:31:37.240
And this will take the
configuration from YAML

00:31:37.240 --> 00:31:41.440
that we just changed and
deploy the resources.

00:31:41.440 --> 00:31:43.990
You can see what
Google Cloud is doing

00:31:43.990 --> 00:31:46.615
by going into your console.

00:31:49.730 --> 00:31:52.790
And as you can see,
the Deployment Manager

00:31:52.790 --> 00:31:56.120
is actually working to
deploy our resources.

00:31:56.120 --> 00:31:58.040
You can see the details
of the resources which

00:31:58.040 --> 00:31:59.690
are provisioning here.

00:31:59.690 --> 00:32:02.010
Now we are deploying a network.

00:32:02.010 --> 00:32:03.740
And as the network
is deployed, we'll

00:32:03.740 --> 00:32:06.410
create some firewall
rules to allow

00:32:06.410 --> 00:32:12.620
communication between the demos
on the nodes that we installed.

00:32:12.620 --> 00:32:15.320
As I mentioned, there is
going to be a Master, a Submit

00:32:15.320 --> 00:32:19.400
node, and then a bunch
of compute resources.

00:32:19.400 --> 00:32:22.430
So while we wait
for this to spin up,

00:32:22.430 --> 00:32:28.520
let me tell you a bit
more about what this is.

00:32:28.520 --> 00:32:33.000
So I went fast over this before.

00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:35.120
The two nodes that
you are deploying

00:32:35.120 --> 00:32:38.300
is the Submit and
Central Manager.

00:32:38.300 --> 00:32:40.460
But the compute
nodes we will deploy

00:32:40.460 --> 00:32:42.840
using what we call a
Managed Instance Group.

00:32:42.840 --> 00:32:46.850
Now, a Managed Instance
Group is a resource in GCP

00:32:46.850 --> 00:32:50.510
that gives you the
ability of creating

00:32:50.510 --> 00:32:52.850
multiple identical resources.

00:32:52.850 --> 00:32:55.370
And so it provides
an interface, an API,

00:32:55.370 --> 00:33:00.560
that allows you to specify
how many nodes of that kind

00:33:00.560 --> 00:33:03.680
you want, and automatically
it will bring it up and down.

00:33:03.680 --> 00:33:05.090
And a Managed
Instance Group also

00:33:05.090 --> 00:33:09.440
does other things, like
rolling updates and more

00:33:09.440 --> 00:33:11.250
than what I'm just telling you.

00:33:11.250 --> 00:33:13.420
But for this demo, we are
only using those APIs.

00:33:16.310 --> 00:33:19.550
So Managed Instance
Group is what

00:33:19.550 --> 00:33:23.420
we are using to actually
implement our autoscaler.

00:33:23.420 --> 00:33:26.570
So our autoscaler, I can
tell you more now about that

00:33:26.570 --> 00:33:29.240
while we wait for
these to come up.

00:33:29.240 --> 00:33:32.390
Well, the deployment has
finished successfully now.

00:33:32.390 --> 00:33:35.360
You see that all of this is up.

00:33:35.360 --> 00:33:41.970
If you go and look at your
VMs, you see them all here.

00:33:41.970 --> 00:33:45.770
What's happening
now inside the VMs,

00:33:45.770 --> 00:33:49.590
a startup script is running,
which is installing HTCondor.

00:33:49.590 --> 00:33:53.153
So let me tell you a bit
more about the autoscaler

00:33:53.153 --> 00:33:54.570
while we wait for
this to install.

00:33:54.570 --> 00:33:57.030
It takes about a
couple of minutes.

00:33:57.030 --> 00:33:59.580
And this is also a
measure of how long

00:33:59.580 --> 00:34:02.170
you have to wait when you
bring up new resources.

00:34:02.170 --> 00:34:04.380
So you autoscale up,
you submit more jobs,

00:34:04.380 --> 00:34:06.030
you autoscale resources up.

00:34:06.030 --> 00:34:09.280
In a couple of minutes, they
are available to run your jobs.

00:34:09.280 --> 00:34:13.020
So how do you scale them up?

00:34:13.020 --> 00:34:19.850
You can go back to the same
page as we were before.

00:34:19.850 --> 00:34:22.380
There is a directory
called autoscaler.

00:34:22.380 --> 00:34:24.790
If you click here,
there is a bunch

00:34:24.790 --> 00:34:27.880
of instructions on how to set
this up so you can do that

00:34:27.880 --> 00:34:28.585
yourself.

00:34:28.585 --> 00:34:29.710
I'll show you how to do it.

00:34:32.639 --> 00:34:39.330
What we are doing, we
have a Python software

00:34:39.330 --> 00:34:42.630
that is called autoscaler,
not surprisingly.

00:34:42.630 --> 00:34:46.170
We just accept a whole bunch of
different arguments documented

00:34:46.170 --> 00:34:47.040
here.

00:34:47.040 --> 00:34:50.670
What we want to do, we
want to go on a node--

00:34:50.670 --> 00:34:52.830
in this case, we'll
use the Submit node--

00:34:52.830 --> 00:34:57.120
and install the
software there, and then

00:34:57.120 --> 00:35:02.800
call the autoscaler with the
appropriate parameters here.

00:35:02.800 --> 00:35:04.690
So let's go through
the deployment.

00:35:04.690 --> 00:35:06.840
Let's see if it has
finished, and then I'll

00:35:06.840 --> 00:35:08.661
show you how to
install the autoscaler.

00:35:11.750 --> 00:35:16.482
So to go to the Submit node,
you can click on the SSH button.

00:35:24.194 --> 00:35:29.030
I'll bring up another
one in case I need it.

00:35:29.030 --> 00:35:31.640
So what's happening
now, the system

00:35:31.640 --> 00:35:35.450
is transferring
keys to the machines

00:35:35.450 --> 00:35:40.620
so that then I can actually log
into the machines using SSH.

00:35:40.620 --> 00:35:42.950
So this is the terminal.

00:35:42.950 --> 00:35:44.580
Let me make it a bit bigger.

00:35:55.260 --> 00:35:59.600
So what you see here is that
we have no jobs currently

00:35:59.600 --> 00:36:04.490
on the system, nothing
is in the queue,

00:36:04.490 --> 00:36:08.270
and that some nodes
are starting to become

00:36:08.270 --> 00:36:10.800
available to report to it.

00:36:10.800 --> 00:36:12.470
So what I want to do--

00:36:12.470 --> 00:36:15.680
in principle, if I submit
jobs, these resources

00:36:15.680 --> 00:36:18.290
will be able to run
the jobs for us.

00:36:18.290 --> 00:36:22.610
What I want to show you now is
how to install the autoscaler.

00:36:22.610 --> 00:36:25.100
So first, I need Git.

00:36:25.100 --> 00:36:25.910
Let me install Git.

00:36:37.190 --> 00:36:41.580
All right, and now the
directory that you want

00:36:41.580 --> 00:36:43.900
is in the same repo
that I showed before.

00:36:43.900 --> 00:36:45.540
So you git clone this.

00:36:53.620 --> 00:36:57.410
OK, this is the same
direct repository

00:36:57.410 --> 00:37:02.010
that I have installed in
my Cloud Shell before.

00:37:02.010 --> 00:37:05.860
Now let's go to the autoscaler.

00:37:05.860 --> 00:37:09.730
You want to run this program.

00:37:09.730 --> 00:37:15.580
And what it will do, since
there is no job in the queue

00:37:15.580 --> 00:37:21.440
and there are resources
available to run jobs,

00:37:21.440 --> 00:37:24.140
if I ran the autoscaler
now, it will say, well,

00:37:24.140 --> 00:37:25.640
I don't need all
these resources,

00:37:25.640 --> 00:37:27.950
let me turn them off.

00:37:27.950 --> 00:37:32.990
So let me cut and paste
this command here.

00:37:42.320 --> 00:37:43.975
This is the one
that I did not want.

00:37:43.975 --> 00:37:47.670
I opened in case I needed it.

00:37:47.670 --> 00:37:48.817
I don't think I need it.

00:37:51.620 --> 00:37:55.120
All right, and that's it.

00:37:55.120 --> 00:37:59.230
The project name, as I said
before, is htcondor-demo.

00:37:59.230 --> 00:38:05.390
And all the other
parameters are fine.

00:38:05.390 --> 00:38:07.730
Now I'm using Verbosity 1.

00:38:07.730 --> 00:38:09.710
What happens is
that the system will

00:38:09.710 --> 00:38:13.340
see that there are more
machines than jobs,

00:38:13.340 --> 00:38:18.060
and it will try to
bring them down.

00:38:18.060 --> 00:38:25.760
Now, one thing that we can
do, we can submit more jobs.

00:38:25.760 --> 00:38:28.019
I have some example jobs here--

00:38:28.019 --> 00:38:28.957
applications.

00:38:33.180 --> 00:38:36.420
This is a simple
piece of SQL that

00:38:36.420 --> 00:38:39.990
calculates the first
n prime numbers,

00:38:39.990 --> 00:38:44.190
just as a way to use
CPU and waste it.

00:38:44.190 --> 00:38:47.650
I'm going to show you
the Condor Submit file.

00:38:47.650 --> 00:38:52.050
This is the Condor Submit
file that specifies

00:38:52.050 --> 00:38:54.270
how to run the program.

00:38:54.270 --> 00:38:56.205
So the executable is prime.

00:38:59.480 --> 00:39:02.940
Yeah, let's calculate the
first 400,000 prime numbers.

00:39:02.940 --> 00:39:05.590
We don't have
anything better to do.

00:39:05.590 --> 00:39:10.040
And so it lets me
run 12 of those.

00:39:10.040 --> 00:39:11.520
All right, so I can submit it.

00:39:22.270 --> 00:39:26.960
All right, so I have
no resources now,

00:39:26.960 --> 00:39:29.610
but I have 12 idle jobs.

00:39:29.610 --> 00:39:36.950
So let me run the resources
with the autoscaler.

00:39:36.950 --> 00:39:38.110
Let me scale them up.

00:39:45.520 --> 00:39:51.200
All right, so what's happening
is that I'm running--

00:39:51.200 --> 00:39:55.180
I have 12 jobs
idle in the queue.

00:39:55.180 --> 00:39:56.950
I need to bring
up three resources

00:39:56.950 --> 00:39:59.850
to actually run these.

00:39:59.850 --> 00:40:03.180
And so what's
happened is that there

00:40:03.180 --> 00:40:05.460
are more resources that
are being brought up

00:40:05.460 --> 00:40:10.060
because I need to run them.

00:40:10.060 --> 00:40:11.950
All right, very good.

00:40:11.950 --> 00:40:14.890
Rather than waiting until
all of this is brought up,

00:40:14.890 --> 00:40:19.180
two minutes, et cetera, let me
summarize what I've told you.

00:40:19.180 --> 00:40:21.970
So we have deployed
an HTCondor system

00:40:21.970 --> 00:40:27.485
in about 10 to 12
minutes in GCP.

00:40:27.485 --> 00:40:29.110
I showed you where
to get the software.

00:40:29.110 --> 00:40:35.050
You have to Google "GitHub
GCP HTCondor," unsurprisingly.

00:40:35.050 --> 00:40:37.570
You find the Google Deployment
Manager scripts there.

00:40:37.570 --> 00:40:42.310
You can go to your Cloud Shell
and git clone that repository.

00:40:42.310 --> 00:40:44.890
If you go into the
HTCondor context,

00:40:44.890 --> 00:40:49.180
you can edit the parameters
of your Condor cluster,

00:40:49.180 --> 00:40:52.720
and then launch the
Deployment Manager so that you

00:40:52.720 --> 00:40:56.970
get your resources up here.

00:40:56.970 --> 00:40:58.540
Once you have your
resources up, you

00:40:58.540 --> 00:41:00.050
can use the cluster that way.

00:41:00.050 --> 00:41:03.430
Or if you want to autoscale it
up and down, like we recommend,

00:41:03.430 --> 00:41:06.270
then you can go to
one of the nodes.

00:41:06.270 --> 00:41:08.500
The Submit node is a good one.

00:41:08.500 --> 00:41:10.540
And then you git
clone the same repo.

00:41:10.540 --> 00:41:13.660
And then you can put the
autoscaler in an automated

00:41:13.660 --> 00:41:14.610
execution fashion.

00:41:14.610 --> 00:41:17.740
Typically, we recommend
to put that as a cron job.

00:41:17.740 --> 00:41:20.660
Maybe every five minutes you
can check, or every two minutes,

00:41:20.660 --> 00:41:22.270
depending on your requirements.

00:41:22.270 --> 00:41:25.690
And automatically, that will
bring up and down the resources

00:41:25.690 --> 00:41:27.580
as you need.

00:41:27.580 --> 00:41:30.700
And with this, I finish my demo.

00:41:30.700 --> 00:41:34.350
[TITLE MUSIC PLAYING]

